# The input image of an enclave image file (EIF) of an MPC party: the RELEASED party image, by digest, plus the deployment's measured configuration. Nothing is built here.
#
#   deploy/images/eif-build.sh --party-image REPO@sha256:<64 hex> --config-dir DIR --out DIR      (the script runs this file and then nitro-cli)
#
# Why the released image and not a rebuild: the PCRs of the EIF are over its content, so the content must be the bytes that were released, signed and
# scanned. A digest names them; a tag does not (eif-build.sh refuses a tag).
# Why the configuration is inside: the party's peer pins, caller pins and admin keys decide who may call it. Inside the image they are measured (PCR0, PCR2);
# outside it, the parent instance could change them (research/TEE.md, "Measure the config"). One deployment, one party, one configuration, one set of PCRs.
#
# The config directory holds party.json (and the certificate and key files it names: generated for the deployment, never in this repository). The base is
# distroless: no shell, nothing the party does not need. Entry point as in the Helm chart (deploy/helm/qv-party): `mpc-party -config /etc/qv/config/party.json`.
#
# NOT here, and needed before an enclave can serve (docs/runbooks/nitro-first-run.md, "What is still missing"): an enclave has no network, so the party's
# listeners must be reached over vsock. The first run uses a small forwarder pair (parent side and enclave side); this file does not add it.
ARG PARTY_IMAGE=required-build-arg-missing
FROM ${PARTY_IMAGE}
COPY --chown=0:0 config/ /etc/qv/config/
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/mpc-party", "-config", "/etc/qv/config/party.json"]
